diagnosis_text:
A complete multi-layer diagnostic found no fault causing the reported intermittent connectivity between PC0 (20.1.1.10) and PC12 (120.1.1.10). Phase 1 reachability showed 0% loss in both directions with sub-millisecond RTT. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL rules, no addressing anomalies, no duplicate MACs, and all 15 devices with correct IPs and default routes. Phase B found no traffic-control shaping (tc_snapshot clean) and confirmed the OSPF+EIGRP hybrid fabric is currently healthy: r11 acts as a redistribution boundary — running OSPF area 0.0.0.3 toward r1 and EIGRP AS 100 toward r12/r13, with `redistribute eigrp` propagating the 120.0.0.0/8 prefix (pc12's subnet) into OSPF so r1 can route to it. EIGRP adjacencies on r11 to both r12 and r13 are UP and stable (all routes Passive, no Active queries, zero interface errors). Phase D found no stress tools, no CPU pressure, and no suspect processes. Direct checks of carrier_changes counters on all path interfaces (r1, r11, r12) each showed exactly 2, the startup-only baseline, proving no link flaps have occurred since the lab began. FRR logs are empty and there are no cron jobs or flap scripts on any device. Several structural OSPF configuration anomalies exist (r8 has an orphaned area 0.0.0.2 not shared by any peer; r13 runs EIGRP only with ospfd absent by design), but none of these create a cyclic disruption mechanism and none are on the pc0→pc12 forwarding path. The user's report of recurring freeze-and-recover cycles every one to two minutes is unconfirmed: there is no mechanism — no flap script, no link instability, no periodic tc rule, no EIGRP SIA event, no OSPF reconvergence — that would produce such behavior. No corrective action is required; the network is operating normally.
